<Docs title="Security model" active="Security model">
  <div class="doc-title"><span class="section-kicker">REFERENCE / 05</span><h1>Security model</h1><p>Security decisions should be visible, testable, and operationally boring.</p></div>
  <h2>Trust boundaries</h2><p>Agent-guard treats the model output, tool adapter, policy store, identity provider, and audit sink as distinct components. The model is never trusted to self-authorize. The adapter is responsible for forwarding the request; the PDP owns the decision.</p>
  <h2>Identity and tokens</h2><p>Use OIDC or API keys for service identity. Delegation uses signed claims with explicit issuer, audience, algorithm, expiry, and key matching. DPoP can bind a token to the caller key and prevent replay across senders.</p>
  <h2>Audit integrity</h2><p>Decision records use canonical JSON and a hash chain. Verify the chain before relying on exported records for incident response. Keep signing secrets and chain secrets in a managed secret store.</p>
  <h2>Report a vulnerability</h2><p>Please do not open a public issue for a security vulnerability. Follow the process in <a class="inline-link" href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/SECURITY.md">SECURITY.md ↗</a> and include reproducible steps, affected versions, and impact.</p>
  <div class="docs-note"><strong>Security is a process.</strong><span>Review policies, rotate keys, and exercise the incident runbook regularly.</span></div>
</Docs>
